MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_001: Oh, Mother. You never change, do you?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_002: What's with that smile, Ultand?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_003: Got a letter from my mother. She's off somewhere\ntrying to earn some money.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_004: Don't get me wrong, she's a skilled worker, but she\ncan be a little, uh, scatterbrained.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_005: She wrote about stepping on the tail of a sleeping\nkusarik. It flipped her cart and made a huge mess.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_006: Hahaha! Somehow that reminds me of you, Ultand.\nBut you said she's off...earning money?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_007: My late grandfather left the family with a lot of debt.\nEveryone, including me, is working to pay it off.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_008: My temple salary is pretty good, but the family debt\nis why I can never put any money aside.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_009: That's rough. But even so, when you talk about your\nfamily, you seem to smile a lot.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_010: Hehe. Yes, we have our problems, and my younger\nsiblings can be a handful.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_011: But after all is said and done, I like my family. And I\nhope we can all be together again someday.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_012: We're a lively bunch, you know? My little brother,\nNoah, reminds me of Cai.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_013: Cai? So I guess he's a cheerful, strong, cool kid, huh?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_014: Compared to Cai, he's more of a rascal. He gets into\na lot of trouble.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_015: And then there's my little sister, Cezarie. She's more\nlike Aurora, I suppose...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_016: My family needs constant looking after, but I'm\nguessing you and yours aren't such a mess.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_017: Well... It isn't exactly like that...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_018: Your turn, then. Tell me about your family. It's not \nfair that I've given you all the dirt on mine.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_019: Oh...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_020: I... I can't talk about family the way you do, Ultand.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_021: I'm sorry.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_022: Wh— Tialla! Wait!
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_C_023: Great. Touched a nerve somehow. Nice\nwork, Ultand.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_001: Come on, now! I'm telling you I'll fix that up for you!\nYou go and pick up some more concoctions!
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_002: Tialla. Do you have a moment? About before...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_003: Oh! Ultand! I'm sorry about earlier.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_004: Wait. Why are you apologizing?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_005: I got sad, and maybe a little jealous, hearing you\ntalk about your family.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_006: But I'd like to tell you about my family, if you still\nwant to hear our story.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_007: Of course, Tialla. Go ahead.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_008: I got this doll from my mother. It was the first and\nlast gift she ever gave me.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_009: When I was little, my parents were in the military,\nand they were hardly ever around.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_010: My grandpa was there for me, but he was always\nbusy too.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_011: Long ago, Grandpa was a master tactician for the\nkingdom of Arago. Every day he'd receive visitors.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_012: His library was full of books, though, and I was\nalways reading.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_013: Always...alone?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_014: Yes, alone, but not necessarily lonely. The world of\nbooks was where I was most comfortable.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_015: I got to the point where I'd read almost everything\non Grandpa's bookshelves.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_016: And that's when it happened—two long, black boxes\nwere brought into the house.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_017: When I saw them, I felt a coldness deep inside\nmy chest.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_018: I'd never seen anything like that before, but \nsomehow I knew in my heart what the boxes were.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_019: Coffins, of course. My mother and father had died.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_020: Tialla...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_021: From then on, Grandpa became kinder. He started\nspending more time with me.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_022: And he began turning visitors away. Before too long,\nno one was coming to see him.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_023: He loaded his whole library, and me, onto a big cart,\nand we left Arago.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_024: And that's the story of me and my family.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_025: Tialla, thank you for sharing that with me.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_026: I don't talk about it much. But, to be honest, this has\ntaken a heavy weight off my chest.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_027: My parents are no longer with us, and I don't want\nCai to have to go through anything like that.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_028: So I'm determined to help Cai win and save Castor!
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_029: I'm with you. I don't want to see Cai or Aurora sad.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_030: I'm cheering for you, Tialla. And I'll be by your side\nevery step of the way.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_B_031: Thanks, Ultand!
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_001: Just to be safe, let's top off the antitoxins... And I'd\nbetter buy some elixirs too!
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_002: Oh! Tialla! Can I see you a minute?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_003: What's going on, Ultand?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_004: Turn. Turn around.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_005: Huh? What?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_006: Your hair's all over the place! Hold still so I can fix it\nfor you.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_007: Oh! I'm OK. With all the running around I have to\ndo, it'll just get messy again.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_008: I see. You have some shopping to do, right?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_009: Well, get going, then. I'm here to help when you\nneed me.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_010: Ultand... Why are you being so...friendly?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_011: I've just been thinking about what I can do for you.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_012: I've decided that we're going to be sisters.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_013: Huh? But you have a sister, and I'm nothing like\nher, right?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_014: That's irrelevant. You're younger than me, Tialla, and\nyou're very cute. You're my cute, little sister.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_015: Cute? But I argue...a lot. And I'm strong-willed, and I\nsay impertinent things. I'm not very graceful, and...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_016: Cute. Little. Sister. And it's only natural for a big\nsister to dote on a younger one.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_017: What I mean is...I'd be very happy if you'd think of\nme as your older sister.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_018: Big sister Ultand.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_019: I'm sorry. It must be strange to hear me spouting all\nthis nonsense so suddenly.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_020: Not at all. Actually, I could get used to this.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_021: Really?! Oh, thank goodness!
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_022: I mean, don't get carried away or anything.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_023: I was nervous to talk to you about this. I don't know\nwhat I'd do if you didn't like the idea.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_024: But we're on the same page, so it all works out.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_025: Now I won't be able to say that Grandpa is my only\nfamily anymore.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_026: If you had been there while I was growing up, our\nhome would have been that much warmer.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_027: Tialla, about your family.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_028: Your late parents, I'm sure they loved you very much.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_029: After all, they gave you a beautiful doll, right?
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_030: Ah...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_031: I'm thinking they did that so you wouldn't feel so\nlonely when they couldn't be there.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_032: You were probably on your parents' minds all \nthe time.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_033: And now I'm on your mind.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_034: Hmhm. You've got that right!
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_035: I'll bet your parents would be so pleased with how\nyou've grown up.
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_036: Ah...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_037: I suppose I need to try and forgive them for leaving\nme... Mother... Father...
MID_SE_Tia_Hur_A_038: That's the way, Tialla.